WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.321 --> 00:00:20.451
The desert wilderness has many names in the Bedu language, my native tongue, because the desert has many

2
00:00:20.511 --> 00:00:27.075
faces. Some parts of the desert have dunes as high as two hundred meters, and other parts are

3
00:00:27.155 --> 00:00:28.196
flat and sandy.

4
00:00:29.644 --> 00:00:34.528
Dry riverbeds can suddenly become raging rivers when the rain falls hard and fast.

5
00:00:36.049 --> 00:00:42.653
After those rare rainstorms, our desert blossoms for a short while into wide carpets of grass and beautiful

6
00:00:42.713 --> 00:00:50.339
wildflowers. My people are always on the move through the deserts of Arabia, tending our camels and goats.

7
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.043
We never stay in one place very long, but we enjoy the region around the oasis at Al-Hasa

8
00:00:57.083 --> 00:00:57.484
the most.

9
00:00:59.008 --> 00:01:02.910
The year is 1670, and my name is Karim Abu Sukar.

10
00:01:04.111 --> 00:01:05.992
I am a member of the Bani Khalid tribe.

11
00:01:06.413 --> 00:01:07.893
It is a great honor to meet you.

12
00:01:09.775 --> 00:01:14.117
My people are called Bedouin, and we spend our lives wandering throughout the desert.

13
00:01:15.558 --> 00:01:19.981
But believe me, my friend, we always know exactly where we are going and why.

14
00:01:20.801 --> 00:01:25.344
When my family travels from water hole to water hole, all our animals travel with us.

15
00:01:27.156 --> 00:01:31.677
My sweet wife, Rana, is the wonderful mother of our son, Falid, who is with our herd of

16
00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:32.397
goats right now.

17
00:01:33.738 --> 00:01:37.399
She takes care of our tent, which is made of goat hair and easily portable.

18
00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:43.500
You can see that the tent is divided into two sections: one for the men and one for

19
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:43.880
the women.

20
00:01:45.341 --> 00:01:50.122
All the cooking utensils are stored on Rana's side of the tent, and we entertain our guests on

21
00:01:50.182 --> 00:01:50.662
my side.

22
00:01:52.043 --> 00:01:54.863
Look at the beautiful curtain Rana has made to divide our tent!

23
00:01:56.304 --> 00:02:00.794
In fact, Rana wove the tent, the carpets we are sitting on, and even my clothes.

24
00:02:02.014 --> 00:02:04.755
Falid and I couldn't live without her and her many talents.

25
00:02:06.436 --> 00:02:10.318
When our family and friends gather to eat and drink coffee, we like to tell stories.

26
00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:17.681
Some tales are about our brave ancestors and their many adventures— about living and fighting in the desert.

27
00:02:18.901 --> 00:02:21.522
Other stories are about a wise fool named Goha.

28
00:02:22.563 --> 00:02:24.243
These stories are always the funniest.

29
00:02:24.824 --> 00:02:25.604
Would you like to hear one?

30
00:02:26.548 --> 00:02:29.170
I think "Goha's Kettle" would be fitting for this occasion.

31
00:02:31.232 --> 00:02:33.814
One day Goha borrowed a large pot from a neighbor.

32
00:02:35.215 --> 00:02:38.778
Several days later he returned it, but with a smaller pot inside.

33
00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:44.543
His neighbor stared at the little pot in surprise and asked Goha, "Where did this come from?"

34
00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:49.107
"The big pot gave birth," Goha replied matter-of-factly.

35
00:02:50.628 --> 00:02:55.232
Laughing at Goha's foolishness and happy at his own good fortune, the neighbor took both pots.

36
00:02:56.916 --> 00:03:00.697
A few weeks later, Goha asked his neighbor whether he could borrow the large pot again.

37
00:03:02.118 --> 00:03:03.938
The neighbor eagerly lent it to him of course.

38
00:03:04.938 --> 00:03:07.599
But months went by, and Goha did not return the pot.

39
00:03:08.539 --> 00:03:10.380
The neighbor became more and more upset.

40
00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.981
At last he went to Goha and asked for it.

41
00:03:13.001 --> 00:03:16.542
"My good friend, something horrible has happened.

42
00:03:17.042 --> 00:03:19.062
Your pot died," replied Goha.

43
00:03:20.143 --> 00:03:22.523
Shocked, his neighbor screamed, "Died?

44
00:03:23.084 --> 00:03:24.464
Since when do kettles die?"

45
00:03:26.396 --> 00:03:28.977
"Ever since they've been able to have children," Goha answered.

46
00:03:29.497 --> 00:03:31.057
That is a good story!

47
00:03:32.418 --> 00:03:36.679
Come, it is getting late, and I must go to my son, who is tending our goats not

48
00:03:36.739 --> 00:03:37.319
far from here.

49
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.541
My father showed me all the best spots for grazing goats, and he knew of these places because

50
00:03:43.601 --> 00:03:46.962
his father had taught him, so my herd is one of the largest.

51
00:03:49.102 --> 00:03:51.303
I have taught Falid to care for the herd as well.

52
00:03:52.683 --> 00:03:54.664
Our black goats survive well in the desert.

53
00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:01.524
They eat almost anything that grows, which is good because very little grows in the desert except for

54
00:04:01.584 --> 00:04:03.486
woody shrubs and an occasional weed.

55
00:04:04.987 --> 00:04:10.071
Black goats are special because they can survive without water for four days, so we can graze our

56
00:04:10.111 --> 00:04:11.713
herd far from the water holes.

57
00:04:13.694 --> 00:04:20.159
Our goats produce milk for our family, and we use everything from the animal—hair, skin, and dung.

58
00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:21.921
Nothing goes to waste!

59
00:04:23.402 --> 00:04:27.857
The goat hair is used to make our tents and blankets, the hides are used to make bags

60
00:04:27.897 --> 00:04:31.440
to carry our water, and the dung is used as fuel for our fires.

61
00:04:32.921 --> 00:04:37.464
When we host a feast, we love to roast a whole goat using our traditional underground oven.

62
00:04:39.105 --> 00:04:42.367
We dig a deep hole and line the inside with clay and stones.

63
00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:47.531
Then we burn wood in the bottom of the hole until it turns to charcoal.

64
00:04:49.633 --> 00:04:54.076
Finally we skewer a skinned goat and suspend it above the charcoal on a spit.

65
00:04:55.584 --> 00:04:59.806
We add some spices and roast the goat for many hours, turning it once in a while.

66
00:05:00.827 --> 00:05:05.809
The aroma is wonderful, and the smoke makes our mouths water as it winds around our camp.

67
00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:12.352
My tribe has existed in this region for a very long time and fought many battles with other

68
00:05:12.392 --> 00:05:19.035
tribes. The grazing lands that we use and the water holes where we drink belong to our tribe—the

69
00:05:19.135 --> 00:05:21.176
ancient and noble Bani Khalid tribe.

70
00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.862
Each tent represents a family, and our tent is part of a larger group of tents called a

71
00:05:26.922 --> 00:05:33.185
camp. All the people who live in our camp are closely related, and everyone in the camp is

72
00:05:33.245 --> 00:05:35.786
related to an even larger group called a clan.

73
00:05:37.527 --> 00:05:41.249
A number of related clans make up an even larger group called a tribe.

74
00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:48.452
Each family, camp, and clan is headed by an elder, and the entire tribe has a leader.

75
00:05:49.868 --> 00:05:54.709
But never forget that the Bedouin are independent people—we like complete freedom.

76
00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.810
We live in a harsh land, so we live simply.

77
00:05:59.951 --> 00:06:05.192
Our people are always courteous and generous to one another and to our guests because we consider it

78
00:06:05.272 --> 00:06:08.193
our duty, and honor is very important to us.

79
00:06:09.613 --> 00:06:10.373
There is my son.

80
00:06:10.914 --> 00:06:12.274
We had better get back to our tent.

81
00:06:12.814 --> 00:06:15.195
Rana is waiting for us with something special to eat.

82
00:06:16.455 --> 00:06:19.656
Come, I know many more Goha stories to make you laugh!
